# Template for secrets/turing-api.env. Real file is gitignored.
# Holds the runtime env for the Turing API (FastAPI service in turing-api/).
#
# Unlike next.env / convex.env, this file is not pushed by `pnpm secrets:sync`
# because the Turing cluster is not a managed platform we can talk to over an
# API. Edit values here, then scp the file to the cluster:
#
#   scp secrets/turing-api.env <user>@<turing-host>:~/tom.quest/turing-api/.env

# Shared API key. Must match TURING_API_KEY in next.env and convex.env.
# Opens the ENTIRE API, write verbs included (POST /allocate, DELETE /jobs/{id},
# POST /sessions/{name}/run — arbitrary commands in a cluster tmux session).
TURING_API_KEY=

# Second, narrower key. Opens ONLY six GETs — /gpu-report, /jobs,
# /sessions/{name}/output and the artifact tree's /cmt-dirs, /cmt-node and
# /cmt-file — via turing-api's verify_read_key. Its holder can
# look at the cluster and cannot act on it.
#
# A DIFFERENT VALUE from TURING_API_KEY, generated for this purpose. It lives
# in exactly two files: here (scp'd to turing-api/.env on the login node) and
# /etc/tts/worker.env on the Jarvis Box, where the tts-turing command and every
# TTS session read it. It is NOT in next.env or convex.env — the web app and
# the GPU-pool reconciler use the full key.
#
# Leaving it empty is safe and is the fail-closed state: the three endpoints
# simply stay full-key-only, and tts-turing reports 401 on every verb but
# `health`. The service must be restarted after this changes.
TURING_READ_KEY=

# Third key, for TTS runner steps. Opens ONLY POST /allocate and DELETE
# /jobs/{id}, via turing-api's verify_launch_key, and only for jobs named
# runner:<runner id>:<label>: every command must run a file inside the CMT
# checkout, one request is capped, and a cancel needs the job to carry the
# runner's name in the live job list. It cannot type into a session or read a
# file, and it cannot touch the GPU pool's gpupool: jobs.
#
# A DIFFERENT VALUE from both keys above. It lives in exactly two files: here
# (scp'd to turing-api/.env on every login node) and /etc/tts/worker.env on
# the Jarvis Box, where only a runner step's process receives it and the
# tts-turing-act command spends it. It is NOT in next.env or convex.env.
#
# Leaving it empty is safe and is the fail-closed state: the two routes stay
# full-key-only, and tts-turing-act refuses every launch and cancel. The
# service must be restarted after this changes.
TURING_RUNNER_KEY=

# Optional: override the default port (8000).
# API_PORT=
